00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.943
YURI GRINSHTEYN: If
you use Prometheus

00:00:01.943 --> 00:00:04.380
to monitor your services
and infrastructure,

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.480
you probably have a
set of configurations

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.180
you've developed over months
or years that serve you really

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:09.870
well.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:13.110
But managing and maintaining
them can be a challenge.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:16.020
Today, I'm going to show you
how to migrate your existing

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.360
Prometheus setup, including
your application monitoring

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.670
configurations, to
Managed Collection,

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.310
and remove the need to
manage any part of Prometheus

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:24.210
at scale.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.580
My name is Yuri, and I'm
an SRE at Google Cloud.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.920
Welcome back to Engineering
for Reliability.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.406
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.830
The Prometheus Operator
is a common way

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:37.290
to manage Prometheus
deployments in production.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.150
The Operator uses
custom resources

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.220
to manage the Prometheus
server itself.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.560
You then define service
monitor resources

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:47.880
to configure how specific
services are monitored.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.540
You can certainly use Prometheus
Operator with Google Cloud's

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:52.070
Managed Service for Prometheus.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:53.915
But migrating to
Managed Collection

00:00:53.915 --> 00:00:55.790
means you won't have to
manage any Prometheus

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:57.303
infrastructure at all.

00:00:57.303 --> 00:00:59.720
Let's have a look at how to
migrate an existing Prometheus

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.790
configuration, managed
by the operator,

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:04.870
to Managed Collection.

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:07.878
Let's start by reviewing
our existing configuration.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:14.400
We have a Prometheus operator
and the Prometheus server

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.220
running in the
monitoring namespace.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:19.830
Our app is running in
the default namespace.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.270
We can confirm
that it's exposing

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.670
Prometheus metrics on
the slash matrix endpoint

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.020
by using port-forward.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.300
And there we are.

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:35.890
We instructed
Prometheus operator

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:40.450
to monitor our app by using
a service monitor resource.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:41.710
Here's our configuration.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.080
And here it is
deployed on the server.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.150
We can use port
forwarding to confirm

00:01:56.150 --> 00:02:00.320
that Prometheus has
our app as a target

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.135
and that it is ingesting
our app's metrics.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.355
At this point, we're ready to
migrate to Managed Collection.

00:02:21.158 --> 00:02:23.700
We're going to enable Managed
Collection in the Cloud console

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:24.460
here.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.560
You can also do this
with a gcloud command.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.945
If you're now running a GKE,
you can also use kubectl.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.030
Once that's done,
we need to instruct

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.920
Prometheus to monitor our app.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.580
To do this, we'll use a pod
monitoring configuration.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.220
Managed collection uses pod
monitoring and resources

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.500
instead of service
monitors to guarantee

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.540
that each collector
only scrapes targets

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.100
on the nodes running now.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.440
This guarantees
scalability by minimizing

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.570
intracluster traffic.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:57.090
The configuration itself is
similar to the service monitor

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:59.580
resource, but you may need
to change some fields.

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.800
We'll link to the documentation
in the episode notes

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.870
so you can be sure
to get this right.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:05.865
Let's apply this configuration.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:20.080
At this point, we can confirm
that our application metrics

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:24.790
are being ingested by
querying them using PromQL

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:25.930
and in Metrics Explorer.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:35.930
And that's how you can
migrate an existing Prometheus

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:40.460
configuration to Google's
Managed Prometheus collection.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:41.960
Thanks so much for
watching today.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:44.450
We learned how to migrate an
existing Prometheus operator

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:47.120
deployment to Google's
managed Prometheus collection

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.760
and how to use a pod
monitoring resource to ensure

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.350
that your service is monitored.

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:54.290
I hope this helps you keep your
services reliable and users

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:55.160
happy.

